 "intro": "Outside Thapsus in Africa (modern Tunisia), in the civil war. You are Julius Caesar, with about 60,000 to 70,000 men in twelve legions. Metellus Scipio and King Juba of Numidia have about 90,000, including 60 elephants, and have come along a narrow strip between a salt marsh and the sea to relieve the town.",
 "debrief": "In reality, Caesar split his Fifth Legion and posted it behind both wings to meet the elephants, with archers and slingers on the flanks. A trumpeter of the Seventh sounded the advance without orders and Caesar ordered the whole line forward (one account says he had just had an epileptic fit). On the right, archers panicked the elephants, which trampled their own troops; on the left, the Fifth stood and drove them back into Scipio’s line with pila and trumpets. Scipio’s army broke, the camps were overrun, and Caesar’s men killed many who tried to surrender, despite his orders. About 10,000 of Scipio’s men died and nearly 1,000 of Caesar’s. It was the last great use of war elephants in the West. The other choices are what-ifs.",
